---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import shedPhoto from '../assets/photos/shed-solar.webp';
---
<Base title="The Land — Windrose Farm" description="37 acres of Arizona high desert at 5,700 feet: shallow rocky soil, 12 inches of rain, and a patient plan to build abundance anyway.">
  <section class="wrap article-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Land</p>
    <h1>Hard ground, honest plans</h1>
    <p class="prose" style="color: var(--ink-soft);">
      Many people would have seen an empty field. We saw possibility — and
      then we did the soil survey, and saw exactly how much work possibility
      was going to take.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="fact-grid">
      <div><span class="num">37</span><span class="label">acres</span></div>
      <div><span class="num">5,700′</span><span class="label">elevation</span></div>
      <div><span class="num">~130</span><span class="label">frost-free days</span></div>
      <div><span class="num">~12″</span><span class="label">rain per year</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Soil</p>
      <h2>The honest starting point</h2>
      <p>
        Per NRCS Web Soil Survey data, our dominant soils are the
        Leanto&#8209;Bisoodi complex and the Mellenthin&#8209;Rock outcrop complex:
        bedrock at 8–20 inches, very low infiltration, high calcium carbonate,
        and roughly 1.2–1.7 inches of available water in the top 60 inches
        of soil. That is not a footnote — it's a structural constraint.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's also the reason the entire approach here is built around
        <em>making</em> soil rather than assuming ground that's ready to plant:
        hügelkultur mounds, contour swales, mulch, and compost. Working with
        hard ground instead of glossing over it is exactly the kind of honesty
        this farm is built on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Water &amp; Soil-Building</p>
    <h2>Catching every drop, building every inch</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Contour swales</strong> are being staked and cut to intercept and slow
        runoff before it sheets off the property, recharging the soil around
        planting zones. The land falls gently east to west — about 40 feet across
        the developed area — and the swales follow that grain.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hügelkultur mounds</strong>, built from on-site ponderosa pine and
        juniper logs, soaked and capped with native soil, create raised,
        moisture-retentive beds that compensate for the shallow, rocky ground.
        The first prototype is going in between the barn and machine shed;
        its lessons will shape the larger orchard-edge system.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rainwater catchment</strong> is planned off every roof into storage
        tanks — meaningful supplementation at twelve inches of rain a year.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Irrigation runs from tank storage on timers</strong>, not directly
        off the well — so the farm survives a weekend of absence without loss.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">The Orchard Site</p>
      <h2>Planting what wants to live here</h2>
      <p>
        The orchard is sited in a former horse exercise area — flat, upslope of
        the barn and shed, next to existing water infrastructure, with a contour
        swale planned along its uphill edge. The ground is likely compacted from
        years of horses (subsoiling required) but potentially fertile from years
        of manure. Every planting was chosen for alkaline, high-desert,
        cold-winter conditions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Asparagus</strong> — the most alkaline-tolerant crop on the list, a 15–20 year productive lifespan, and the earliest harvest of the year</li>
        <li><strong>Jujube</strong> — the standout pick; evolved for exactly these conditions, near-zero-maintenance once established, 50+ year lifespan</li>
        <li><strong>Apples</strong> — cold-hardy varieties suited to the elevation</li>
        <li><strong>Seaberry, elderberry &amp; aronia</strong> — an alkaline-tolerant berry layer</li>
        <li><strong>Lavender</strong> — thrives in alkaline, well-drained soil, and doubles as bee forage</li>
        <li><strong>Wild harvest</strong> — prickly pear and pinyon pine already grow here on their own</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Infrastructure</p>
    <h2>The bones of the place</h2>
    <figure class="snapshot">
      <Image
        src={shedPhoto}
        alt="The red machine shed with a row of solar panels on its green metal roof, under a clear high-desert sky"
        widths={[640, 1024, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 72rem) 100vw, 66ch"
      />
      <figcaption>The machine shed, wearing the solar array that powers the working core of the farm.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Off-grid solar</strong> centered on the machine shed — an EG4 18kPV inverter and 48V all-weather battery bank, with a buried feeder planned out to the barn</li>
      <li><strong>Well &amp; water distribution</strong>, rebuilt from the ground up, feeding two 2,500-gallon storage tanks</li>
      <li><strong>Barn</strong> with slab and infrastructure underway</li>
      <li><strong>Machine shed</strong>, established and anchoring the working core of the property</li>
      <li><strong>Kubota BX23S tractor</strong> for the land work — subsoiling, mound-building, grading</li>
      <li><strong>Starlink</strong>, because even a homestead measured in decades likes a weather radar</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The Roadmap</p>
      <h2>Five phases, no hurry</h2>
      <ol class="phase-list">
        <li>
          <b>Foundational work</b>
          <p>Finish the well system, soil testing, stake and cut swales, build the first hügelkultur mound, fence the initial production zone.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Observation &amp; prep</b>
          <p>Watch real water flow during monsoon storms and adjust. Consolidate barn-floor infrastructure. Fall soil prep — elemental sulfur, compost, mulch — and order bare-root orchard stock.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>First plantings</b>
          <p>Asparagus, jujube, and apples go in, watered by drip irrigation from tank storage.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Expansion — years 2–3</b>
          <p>The berry layer and lavender. Phased perimeter fencing, native windbreaks, and chickens as orchard pest control once fencing and water are solid.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Long-term — years 3–10</b>
          <p>Rainwater catchment off every roof. Bees, once orchard and lavender bloom can support them. Continued soil-building until the production zone runs largely on mulch and monsoon capture. Eventually, a dwelling and full energy independence.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
